topPRs.body:
Stacked on #24249; review that first. This PR's own change is the last commit.

After #24249 caps the span of games the proposer creates, a create that is reorged out after its receipt can stop creation until a restart. The receipt arms the creation guard, the game never reaches the cache, and every later plan stays at or below the guarded timestamp. Before #24249, the jump to the safe head eventually got past the guard.

The guard now records the game's factory uuid inputs (root claim and extra data) along with its address. When the guard holds and its game is neither cached nor pending, the proposer reads the signer's nonces and then looks the uuid up on the factory at the latest block:

- a transaction of ours is still pending: keep the guard (the rule that already clears an in-flight creation record);
- the uuid is free: release the guard;
- the uuid belongs to another creator: release the guard, and the collision walk steps past that copy;
- the uuid is ours at another address: keep the guard and move it to that address.

The last case exists because the factory derives game addresses from the parent block hash, so a reorged create that is re-included lands at a new address under the same uuid. Reading the nonces first means a create that lands between the two reads still shows up in the lookup.

No effect on operators beyond creation resuming after such a reorg. While the guard holds and its game is not yet cached, planning makes one or two extra L1 reads per loop iteration.

Closes https://github.com/ethereum-optimism/proofs-team/issues/69
